# 9. AUTHENTICATION AND SECURITY ARCHITECTURE

## 9.1 Authentication flow

Supabase Auth is the approved identity provider.

Authentication flow:

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant U as User
    participant B as Browser
    participant A as MOMENT Server
    participant S as Supabase Auth
    participant D as PostgreSQL

    U->>B: Submit login credentials
    B->>S: Authentication request
    S-->>B: Auth result and session
    B->>A: Request protected resource
    A->>S: Verify session
    S-->>A: Verified identity
    A->>D: Execute authorised operation
    D-->>A: Result
    A-->>B: Application response
    B-->>U: Updated interface
```

The actual session transport and cookie strategy must be verified against the installed Supabase and Next.js integration versions.

## 9.2 Security boundaries

The browser is untrusted.

Never trust:

- Client-provided role names.
- Client-provided ownership identifiers.
- Client-provided scores.
- Client-provided vote counts.
- Client-provided moderation privileges.
- Client-side validation alone.

All sensitive decisions must be enforced by trusted server operations and database policies.

## 9.3 Privileged credentials

Supabase service-role credentials must remain server-side.

They must never be:

- Embedded in browser bundles.
- Returned through API responses.
- Logged.
- Committed to Git.
- Included in public environment variables.

Use least-privilege database access wherever possible.

## 9.4 Authorisation

The system must support:

- Guest.
- Registered user.
- Moderator.
- Administrator.
- Super administrator.

Every privileged operation must verify the user's actual permissions.

No user may assign privileges to themselves through a profile update or direct API request.

## 9.5 Row Level Security

RLS is a mandatory database security layer.

The detailed table-by-table policies, SQL and test scenarios will be defined in Document 02.

Application-layer authorisation and database-layer authorisation must complement one another.

