# 16. DEPLOYMENT ARCHITECTURE

## 16.1 Environments

Maintain three distinct environments:

1. Local development.
2. Staging.
3. Production.

Each environment must have independently managed configuration and credentials.

Never use production credentials for ordinary development or testing.

## 16.2 Deployment topology

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    DEV[Developers and CI]
    GIT[Git Repository]
    CI[Automated CI Pipeline]

    DEV --> GIT
    GIT --> CI

    CI -->|Validated Build| STAGE[Staging Environment]
    STAGE --> QA[Automated and Manual Verification]
    QA -->|Approved Release| PROD[Production Environment]

    subgraph PROD["Production"]
        V[Vercel Application]
        S[Supabase Project]
        M[Sentry Monitoring]
        V <--> S
        V --> M
    end
```

## 16.3 CI/CD requirements

The pipeline must verify:

- Dependency installation.
- Formatting.
- Linting.
- Type checking.
- Unit tests.
- Integration tests.
- Build.
- Security checks.
- Migration validation.

Critical failures must block release.

## 16.4 Database migrations

All schema modifications must be version-controlled.

Production migrations require review and approval.

The application must not automatically perform destructive schema changes during normal startup.

## 16.5 Recovery

Document:

- Application rollback.
- Database backup.
- Database restoration.
- Migration recovery.
- Credential rotation.
- Incident response.

Never assume a database rollback is safe when it may discard user-generated data.

